Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Unknown
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/13/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal_live_action; contemporary Midwest family drama, naturalistic faces, fabric weave, and available light

FADE IN:

EXT. KIRK STREET - DAY

A five-story brick walk-up hunches over a Milwaukee sidewalk. Fire-escape stairs stripe the facade.

THE NARRATOR, late twenties, lean working-class build, short brown hair, worn jacket and jeans, hauls two plastic grocery bags toward the door. Behind him, NICK, early thirties, tall and thick as a wrecking ball, reddish-brown hair uncombed, a scar on his chin and another over his left eyebrow, puzzle-piece face held together by old brawls, carries six bags like they weigh nothing.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Big brother was mine. Not that stupid book from junior high. My real Big Brother.

Nick shoulders the street door without breaking stride. People on the sidewalk notice him and step aside.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick was the guy everybody was scared of. He had a pair on him the size of steel wrecking balls.

INT. KIRK STREET STAIRWELL - DAY

Five flights of scuffed linoleum. Nick takes them two at a time. The Narrator lags.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
We lived downtown on Kirk Street after dad took off when I was about eight. Fifth-floor walk-up.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

A cramped railroad flat. Brown overstuffed chair, rabbit-ear television, a blue afghan, pill bottles on the kitchen counter. MA, sixties, dull brown hair, tired brown eyes, janitor-stooped shoulders, cardigan over a housedress, sits in the chair with the television glow on her face.

Nick dumps the bags. The Narrator rubs his hands.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Luckily, when I went to the store with Nick, he could load up about six bags and take them up in one trip.

Nick checks the milk, nods once at Ma, and turns for the door.

INT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

Iron and liniment. A man mid-set sees Nick coming, drops the bar, and offers the bench. Nick swaps in a fifty-pound bar and curls like it is a warm-up. The Narrator pretends to stretch.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I'm not too shabby, but I ain't gonna curl no fifty pounds as a warm-up. That's okay. Nick is my brother. I know he'll stand up for me no matter what.

EXT. KIRK STREET ALLEY - DAY

Dented garbage-can lids mark bases. A scuffed kickball sits in a puddle. The alley is empty.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Milwaukee is where we lived all our lives. We used to play kickball back here. Little Mark Conner kicked a ball through old Mrs. Engel's kitchen. Nick said he was the one who did it.

The Narrator stands where home plate used to be.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Ma paid for the window. A week later Nick gave her sixty bucks. He never told her it was Mark. None of us did.

EXT. KIRK STREET - DUSK

Nick's 1980 Pontiac Trans Am idles at the curb, burnt orange with yellow flames, T-top, the big eagle on the hood. The Narrator runs a hand along the flame job. Nick grins around a cigarette.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
He got a great deal from some guy in Chicago. I love riding in that thing.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Ma's bedroom door is shut. Nick stands in the living room. The Narrator hovers by the kitchen.

NICK
What's Ma doin' back in the bedroom at this time of day?

THE NARRATOR
I don't know, why?

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. That's how old people go downhill. They start sleepin' all day.

THE NARRATOR
I'll do a better job. I'll make her sit out here with me.

NICK
That's more like it.

Nick leaves. The Narrator looks at the shut door, then at the television already tuned to a game show Ma is not watching.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
All she wants to do is watch game shows and cooking channels. Ma worked years as a janitor. She started having back problems and had to take retirement. Between her and me with my pizza job money, we make ends meet.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Nick fills the kitchen doorway. The Narrator washes a pan.

NICK
You don't need a degree to get a good job. All you need is to know somebody.

THE NARRATOR
Then talk to your boss. Get me on at the metal factory.

Nick's mouth flattens.

NICK
What kind of shit do you want to take at school anyway?

THE NARRATOR
I don't know. Something real.

NICK
The real world is here. You're not gonna be a doctor or lawyer. Hell, you're too old anyway.

The Narrator stares at the dishwater.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick is right. I am too old. Besides, I have to take care of Ma. I do like reading though. I don't tell nobody that.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR SIDEWALK - DAY

The Narrator walks his bike to the rack, pizza-shop polo under the jacket. A girl catches her toe on the cement and falls into his shoulder.

SIONNA, mid-twenties, slim, pale heart-shaped face, short brown hair, light-blue eyes like sky before sunrise, black cheap V-neck, jeans, blue tennis shoes, a fish-symbol necklace, a big backpack.

THE NARRATOR
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

They look at each other and do not know what to say.

THE NARRATOR
I work here. I'm just going to work. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

THE NARRATOR
Sionna? That's different. You want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks, I don't eat pizza. But if you want to go have a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

He takes her number with fingers that do not quite work. She walks away and looks back over her shoulder.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
No girl has ever been interested in me. She was not cute. She was pretty. It was like I knew her. Intense. Yea, that's it.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Pill bottles lined on the counter. Ma, eyes glassy and empty, rummages, mumbling. The Narrator comes in still thinking about Sionna.

THE NARRATOR
Ma, what's the matter?

MA
I can't find my pain pills. Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

THE NARRATOR
Ma, what are you talking about? We don't go to church anymore. Mrs. Canasta lives down one level. Your pills are right in front of you.

Ma gives him a look he has never seen. Empty. Then she snaps back.

MA
Oh, right, I am so sorry. I see them right here.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Wow, she was losing it. I would have to tell Nick.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

Packers and Bears on a high television. Pool table, smoke. The Narrator sits over a beer. Nick slaps him on the back hard enough to spill foam.

NICK
Hey, little brother. What's shakin'?

THE NARRATOR
I don't know. Nothin'.

NICK
What's the matter? I can tell you're hidin' something.

The Narrator tells him about Ma. Nick's face goes red.

NICK
I knew she would start going crazy cooped up in that place all day. Why don't you take her to the store or something? Holy Christ, do something with her.

THE NARRATOR
I just needed to get away from her for a while, that's all.

Nick gives him a look like he wants to punch him in the face.

NICK
They take every last dime out of these old people, then leave the family in debt. Is that what you want?

THE NARRATOR
Of course not.

NICK
Then get home. Watch it with her. Get her out tomorrow.

THE NARRATOR
That's what I'll do.

The Narrator pays and stands.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Sometimes I really hate Nick inside. He's done so much for us. I would never tell anybody this.

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREET - NIGHT

Cold enough to sting. Across the street, a stone library, lights still on.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Who's gonna know anyway? Ma can barely remember. Nick won't know.

He cuts across.

INT. MILWAUKEE LIBRARY - NIGHT

High windows, wood floors a hundred years old. It smells of old paper. The Narrator looks like he wandered into a church.

THE NARRATOR
How do I take a book home from here?

The librarian slides a form across. He writes his name. She hands him a temporary paper card.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was like I could get anything in here for free. Who needed school?

He stops in classic literature and pulls Wuthering Heights.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Shit, I missed the whole game.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Ma already in bed. The Narrator sits under a cheap lamp and opens the book.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
So far it was a little like a soap opera. I got the feeling these two really loved each other. This reminded me of Sionna. I needed to call her in the morning.

INT. KIRK STREET BEDROOM - NIGHT

The Narrator asleep. His face twitches.

DISSOLVE TO:

EXT. ENGLISH MOOR - DAY

Dream light, oversaturated. A woman with Sionna's pale face and light-blue eyes wears old riding clothes, running happy through heather on a horse.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Somehow I mixed up Sionna and Catherine. I just got this feeling I would end up feeling for her like Heathcliff.

The horse and the woman recede into fog.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

The Narrator on the wall phone, cord stretched. His hands shake.

THE NARRATOR
Hi. Sionna? This is the guy that got your number outside of the pizzeria.

SIONNA (V.O.)
Of course I do. How are you doing?

THE NARRATOR
Fine, ummm, I was wondering if you want to go for that cup of coffee.

SIONNA (V.O.)
Absolutely.

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

College backpacks, textbooks. The Narrator feels too old the second he walks in. Sionna waits at a small back table, washed-out jeans, long-sleeved loose green hooded top. He sees the watery blue-crayon eyes.

THE NARRATOR
You ever go to school?

SIONNA
I have a degree in English. I love to read and write stories. What are you reading right now?

THE NARRATOR
Wuthering Heights. This guy and girl grew apart but were really in love.

SIONNA
Yea, I know. I read it.

She talks about ghosts, the moors. A love that never changes destroys Heathcliff.

SIONNA (CONT'D)
A love that never changes will destroy you.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It reminded me of Nick. Strong and unwavering. I wondered if it would destroy him too.

Something in him comes awake. They make another date. Dinner. Friday.

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREET - DAY

The Narrator walks toward a bus stop, stunned and taller somehow.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Maybe that was Ma's problem. Dad left, and Ma never moved on. It just ended up destroying her. There ain't no way I could ever forgive him.

INT. KIRK STREET BEDROOM - NIGHT

The Narrator bolts upright, sweating, chest pounding.

THE NARRATOR
Hold!

He sits on the edge of the bed, staring at his hands.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I hardly ever dreamed anything. I remember it all though.

EXT. ANCIENT FOREST - DAY

Dream. Thick woods, moss on oaks. The Narrator rides close behind a woman on a brown dappled mare. Fine white linen sleeves. A red embroidered tunic. Her long brown hair hangs to the small of her back. Rose and warm grass.

They break into an open field. He jumps down and lifts her. Heart-shaped fair face. Lightest blue eyes. First kiss. He surrenders.

The image holds on those eyes.

INT. KIRK STREET BEDROOM - NIGHT

He presses his palms to his eyes.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
The weird thing was the clothes. It was like it was my life for real. It was Sionna, but in a different place. Did I insert these people into Wuthering Heights? It had to be make-believe. Didn't it?

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

Same watery eyes. Sionna listens to the dream and does not laugh.

SIONNA
Some people think dreaming is real, and being awake is not.

THE NARRATOR
I don't get that. I'm sitting in a chair. How could all this be just fake?

SIONNA
It's not fake. It just won't last more than the present moment. Maybe you were dreaming about something from a past life.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
If that was a past life, I wish I could go back there. Especially the girl.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - DAY

A purple rectangle mat. The Narrator in a t-shirt and shorts. Sionna on the next mat, calm, light-blue eyes closed. He shakes in a down-dog. Afterward he lies in corpse pose and something unclenches.

SIONNA
You were really great at that.

THE NARRATOR
Are you kidding me?

SIONNA
No. You caught on very quickly. You did like it, didn't you?

THE NARRATOR
Um, yeah. Yeah! I did like it. I just felt so dumb.

SIONNA
That's pretty common. Your practice is your practice. That's why they call it practice.

They both smile.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - NIGHT

Ovens glowing. The Narrator stretches dough and does not hate it. An asshole customer snaps fingers. He just nods.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Making pizzas didn't bother me. I was just happy to be there. That has never happened, unless you count payday.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Nick stomps, thick and red-faced, fists opening and closing. Ma sits in her chair as if nothing is up.

THE NARRATOR
What's going on Nick?

NICK
Ma missed her doctor's appointment yesterday, you ass. Where the hell were you?

THE NARRATOR
I totally forgot. I'm sorry. I was with someone.

NICK
With someone? What the hell is that? With who?

THE NARRATOR
I met a girl, and we had a couple dates. That's all.

Nick stares, black-eyed.

NICK
Oh, great. A girl. Don't you think Ma deserves better than that?

THE NARRATOR
I said I was sorry!

Nick slams the door in his face.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I hardly ever get into it with Nick. You just don't know what he is going to do. One time in high school he sprayed lighter fluid on a kid's yard and set it on fire. Everybody is afraid of Nick.

INT. MILWAUKEE LIBRARY - DAY

The Narrator reads about attachment, suffering, the present moment.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It said we look for happiness in the outside world. Life is suffering, and the cause is the search for things. Through meditation we can live only for the present. That was really cool.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

The Narrator on the wall phone. Ma's game show in the other room.

THE NARRATOR
I wish you'd finally come over. Meet my Ma.

SIONNA (V.O.)
Are you sure? It's kind of a big deal to meet your mother.

THE NARRATOR
Hell, she probably won't even remember you in the morning. I read a book about Buddhism. I think I want to try meditating sometime.

SIONNA (V.O.)
You've already tried it, silly. Savasana. Corpse pose.

He hangs up, a little ready.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Ma in the chair, game show colors on her face.

THE NARRATOR
How's it goin' Ma?

MA
What? Oh, it's you.

That funny confused look, as if she does not know him.

THE NARRATOR
Are you okay Ma?

MA
Oh, I don't know. It just feels like my heart is going to jump out of my chest. I'm a little dizzy too.

He reaches for the phone.

INT. DR. CARSON'S OFFICE - DAY

DR. CARSON, middle-aged, serious, white coat, meets the Narrator's eye. Ma is already in another room.

DR. CARSON
Hello, I'm Dr. Carson. You're the younger son, right?

THE NARRATOR
Yeah, that's right. Uh, how's she doin' Doc?

DR. CARSON
Your mother is fine. I want to talk to you about her blood pressure. It's dangerously high. I'm afraid of the risk of a stroke. Take her directly to the emergency room.

THE NARRATOR
Whoa man. Are you sure it's that bad?

DR. CARSON
She has been confused. And she experienced chest pressure this afternoon?

THE NARRATOR
Yeah. She hasn't been acting too normal lately.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

Three days later. Ma dozes, smaller in the bed. SIONNA meets the Narrator; they walk the corridor. Her eyes sparkle like light-blue diamonds.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
She made me feel excited and calm all at the same time. I think I loved her. I just hadn't told her yet.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

The Narrator asleep in the chair. Ma dozing. His face twists.

EXT. MEDIEVAL BATTLEFIELD - DAY

Dream. Chaos. Men screaming. Blood on the ground. The Narrator stands over a dying man. Blood bubbles from the mouth.

The dying man shapes one word.

DYING MAN
Save her.

The Narrator turns. In the middle of dozens of falling men, Nick runs toward him, thick, fire-eyed.

THE NARRATOR
Nick! Nick! Thank God you're here! I thought I was going to die.

NICK
That you are, little brother.

Nick drives the longest knife imaginable into his chest.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

The Narrator wakes gasping. Ma still dozes.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Why would Nick want to kill me? I felt betrayed. That had to be the worst betrayal of all.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

A blue plastic weekly pill box on the table. Sionna helped buy it. Ma eyes it like it might bite.

THE NARRATOR
See Ma, every day of the week. Separations for morning and night.

MA
Oh my Lord, that looks a little overwhelming.

THE NARRATOR
No worries. I'm goin' to be here. Right pill, right time.

MA
You're such a good boy. You and Nick are like a God-send.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
You two? Like Nick was ever around anymore. That was a laugh.

The door opens. Nick, beet-red, prison-break eyes, big boots. He laughs too loud.

NICK
What's up little Bro? You got Ma all tucked in? Man, you shoulda been a nurse!

THE NARRATOR
Knock it off man. Somebody has to make sure she stays on track. Where have you been?

NICK
Don't start giving ME shit Bro. I was at work. More than you can say for that bullshit pizza parlor.

THE NARRATOR
It's eight o'clock. Ma's been home for like four hours.

Nick drops into the brown chair. The Narrator hands him the blue box.

NICK
Oh, holy shit! Aren't you nurse Nancy! How did you come up with this little idea?

THE NARRATOR
If you must know, Sionna helped me. She's really smart.

NICK
This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger.

THE NARRATOR
Stop it Nick! You haven't even met her. Give her a fuckin' break!

Nick stands, a giant red Viking.

NICK
Don't you talk to me like that, you little bastard.

THE NARRATOR
I'm not a bastard!

NICK
How the hell do you know? You sure are a giant pussy.

THE NARRATOR
You're so Goddamn hateful. Was Ma on crack or somethin' when she was pregnant with you?

Nick's hand slams across the Narrator's head. The left ear rings.

NICK
Don't you EVER talk that way about MY mother you little fuck.

Nick stomps out. The door slams.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I had never been in a screaming fight with Nick. In that split second, I knew I never wanted Sionna to meet that prick.

EXT. SCHOOLYARD - DAY

Late spring, years earlier. YOUNG NARRATOR, about nine, folded over laughing until he cannot breathe. A boy his age laughs with him.

YOUNG NARRATOR
You HAVE to learn some responsibility!

They cry it in unison, mocking a teacher.

YOUNG NICK walks up, eleven or twelve, already tall, reddish-brown hair in a devilish mess.

YOUNG NICK
What are you two numbskulls doin'?

YOUNG NARRATOR
Just the usual crap from Mrs. Stark.

Young Nick slaps his shoulder.

YOUNG NICK
Little Bro, you better start listening. Ma is pissed already.

MRS. STARK strides up, glasses on a beaded necklace.

MRS. STARK
Mr. Olson! Would you like to explain yourself before I send you in to Mr. Pauls?

YOUNG NICK
Um, sorry Mrs. Stark. He didn't mean anything. I'll talk to him.

MRS. STARK
Well, Nick, I know you're a good student. YOU need to follow your brother's example.

She marches off.

YOUNG NARRATOR
Nick, um thanks man. You saved my skin.

Young Nick smirks a warning and walks toward a knot of sixth-graders.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
If they only knew the kind of stuff he did. He stole. He was a bully. It was like Nick lived in a bubble.

INT. YOUNG NARRATOR'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

Young Nick musses the Young Narrator's hair.

YOUNG NICK
You're such a numbskull, man. I'll always take care of you. Don't forget that.

YOUNG NARRATOR
I won't Nick. I won't.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

The Narrator stares out the front window. A slight grin.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
That must have been what triggered it. He had always been the alpha dog. Both of them completed me. He was the leader. She was the soft part. Without both of them, who would I be?

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Months later. Nick in big boots and a brown suede jacket kisses Ma, who is watching a cop show. He turns on the Narrator.

NICK
So, what's up with you?

THE NARRATOR
Ah, not much. Just working and thinking about going to school.

Nick stares as if Ma had said she was pregnant.

NICK
Going to school? How the hell are you going to pay for that? And what for?

THE NARRATOR
Community college. Bachelor's in business. Maybe manage a restaurant.

NICK
How the hell will you have time to watch Ma if you're screwing around with those pencil-pushers?

THE NARRATOR
Sionna told me I could qualify for a low-interest student loan. They gave me the money.

NICK
Whoa. You are letting this chick tell you what to do.

THE NARRATOR
I don't want to work in a pizza place my whole life. This was my idea. It would mean a lot if you could just be on my side for once.

NICK
Be on your side? How many times have I saved your ass?

Ma looks up and silently begs them not to argue.

NICK
Donny Amstel. Locker room. Threatening your bike. I followed him home. I shoved in his front door. I told him our family had mafia connections. He believed me.

THE NARRATOR
It did work Nick. You are something else man.

Nick hits his arm lightly.

NICK
You're not just my brother man, you're my best friend. Nothin' is ever gonna come between us. Not school, not Ma, and not this girl. Bro's before ho's.

THE NARRATOR
Sure Nick. Sure. I get you man.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Who the hell is he to call my girlfriend a ho? I just kept it under wraps.

EXT. KIRK STREET - DAY

Blue sky, warm. Kids skateboard outside a hardware store. One looks at the Narrator and Sionna and they all laugh. The Narrator bristles. Sionna just smiles.

THE NARRATOR
What the hell is so funny about us?

SIONNA
Nothing, unless you're thirteen.

THE NARRATOR
I feel like I want to punch that kid. Doesn't that piss you off?

SIONNA
No. Why do you let other people bother you like that? Only when we become masters of ourselves can we really deal with the world.

He leans down and kisses her. Slow motion. No one else matters.

SIONNA
Love is the universal language, and we know how to speak it.

THE NARRATOR
You're the only one I can speak it to, I guess.

SIONNA
We have to learn how to speak it to everyone. Even those kids. Loving kindness.

THE NARRATOR
If my girlfriend is loving everyone, I think I'm going to need a new one.

SIONNA
I could never love anyone like I love you.

THE NARRATOR
I could never love anyone the way I love you either.

They walk. He will remember her face for the rest of his life.

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Werner Street. High ceilings beyond the mess. Nick on the couch, laughing so hard he might fall. BOB, flannel, small beer belly, holds a ceramic pipe. SAMMY drinks a Bud. The Narrator sits in a rocking chair.

BOB
This girlfriend of mine used to be a freak, but she won't put out anymore.

NICK
Aint' many good looking girls in Milwaukee. Aint' that right Bro? Oh wait. He's got the perfect girlfriend. She has him wrapped right now.

BOB
Are you being pussy whipped?

THE NARRATOR
You three wouldn't recognize class and intelligence if it jumped up and bit your ass.

They howl.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick pretends to be understanding alone, then calls me out. All that best-friend crap just doesn't hold water.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

Saturday packed. Smoke, cue sticks. AC/DC from the speakers. The Narrator has not been here in six months. He orders a Bud beside Nick's Jack and Coke.

A waitress passes. Nick laughs about Tonya. Then he goes still, looking into the pool room.

NICK
That looks like Lindsey over there. Who's she with? Who's that asshole?

THE NARRATOR
You broke up with her. Drop it man.

NICK
Kiss my ass! Did I ask your opinion?

Nick walks. Bob, Sammy, and the Narrator follow. LINDSEY, pretty, light blonde hair, pink sweater and boots, stands with DARK MAN, black wavy hair, caterpillar brows.

NICK
What's up Lindsey.

LINDSEY
Oh, hi Nick. Not much.

DARK MAN
Who is this?

LINDSEY
Oh, just an old friend.

The room quiets.

NICK
Where did you meet this one?

LINDSEY
Nick, why does it matter to you?

THE NARRATOR
Nick. Leave it alone.

Nick shoves him aside.

DARK MAN
What the fuck is wrong with you? You're in my face like a little bitch!

Nick's nostrils flare. The Narrator steps closer than he has ever dared.

THE NARRATOR
Lindsey can see whoever she wants. Leave it.

NICK
Keep out of it! You're going to be next if you don't get out of my face asshole.

THE NARRATOR
Stop it! You're crazy as shit! No one cares about your ego Nick!

Time slows. Nick's right fist draws back toward the Narrator's face. Joe and a bouncer rush in.

LINDSEY
No! Stop!

Nick head-butts the bouncer and tears free. The Narrator is already running.

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREET - NIGHT

The Narrator runs like a bear is chasing him, lungs burning. He is not running home.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

An old duplex living room, green suede sofa. Sionna opens her arms. The Narrator pours out Sammy, Bob, the fist.

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you.

THE NARRATOR
Yes, he did.

SIONNA
No one does that. No one who really cares about you. He is a thorn. A controlling ass.

She strokes his face.

SIONNA (CONT'D)
You have been to yoga and meditation half a year. You are becoming a bravely compassionate being.

THE NARRATOR
My purpose....what is my purpose?

SIONNA
I don't know. I suppose we won't know until life is over.

THE NARRATOR
Isn't that a little too late?

She cups his jaw.

SIONNA
You're exhausted. Stay with me tonight.

THE NARRATOR
I have to go check on Ma.

SIONNA
That is exactly where Nick will go. Your Ma is okay for one night.

He lies with his head in her lap.

THE NARRATOR
The first time I met you, it was like I had finally come home.

SIONNA
Me too honey. Me too. The purpose of your life is to find the true self within you, and to honor it.

THE NARRATOR
I hope I can.

His eyes close.

EXT. DREAM LAKE - DAY

Black and white at first. The Narrator in muddy tunic, exhausted. He cups water from the reeds. From the middle of the lake Sionna rises, long brown hair, light-blue eyes, white fabric swirling, dry in the water.

Color slams in. They embrace. Then Nick emerges from the woods as a giant, teeth huge, eyes of fire, a mace whirling.

Sionna spreads her sleeves and flies. An invisible force shoves Nick back. Water against fire. He shrinks and vanishes. She smiles. The Narrator kisses real lips.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

He bolts upright on the green sofa. Three a.m.

THE NARRATOR
I had the craziest dream. You were an angel. You protected us from Nick.

SIONNA
Well, that sounds about right.

They shuffle to her bed.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Months of grind. Ma in her afghan. Sionna reads to her; Ma forgets the story and remembers the feeling. The Narrator fills the blue pill box with Sionna watching.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick was avoiding us. When Ma asked, I told her poker or the gym. I couldn't bear her thinking badly of him.

INT. KIRK STREET BEDROOM - DAY

The Narrator at the computer, a short paper on Descartes. The doorbell. Sionna in the doorway, hair pinned up, flowered shirt, exhausted and regal. She flops on the bed.

SIONNA
Geez, what a day.

He watches her mouth move like a vacation.

THE NARRATOR
I love you.

SIONNA
Have you been listening to anything I said?

THE NARRATOR
Every word.

He jumps on her. Then:

SIONNA
So, what's your paper about?

THE NARRATOR
Dualism and Descartes.

SIONNA
Is this bed here when you don't see it?

THE NARRATOR
Does a tree that falls in the forest make a sound if no one hears it?

SIONNA
Do I exist if you don't see me?

THE NARRATOR
Don't fuck with my head. I can't take it anymore.

She cradles his head.

SIONNA
You know my parents were hippies, right? Mom said she got pregnant with me at Woodstock. Dad went by Mushroom. Mom went by Jasmine. That's how I got Sionna. Pronounced like Shanna. Matron of the river Shannon.

THE NARRATOR
Where are they now?

SIONNA
Where all old hippies go. To the farm. Door County.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - DAY

Lumpy chocolate couch. Sionna finds a four-inch blue ceramic pipe in a little red oriental box. She pinches pot from a baggie. They laugh and cough. Green Day on the stereo.

THE NARRATOR
What do you think this song means?

SIONNA
None of us knows what will happen. We have to make the best of things.

THE NARRATOR
I think I'm stoned. Ha ha ha ha.

SIONNA
I can sometimes see people's auras. You are a beautiful color of blue. Loyalty. You have been loyal to your Ma and your brother your entire life.

EXT. DAIRYLAND ICE CREAM - DAY

Soft-serve on a curved cement bench. Then BOB is suddenly there, flannel untucked, Marlboro already coming out.

BOB
Wow, man! Haven't seen you since the big brawl at Joe's.

THE NARRATOR
Hey man. Bob, this is my girlfriend Sionna.

They shake. Bob chuckles.

BOB
Did you know your boyfriend is famous? He single-handedly conquered the BEAST. You moved like Bruce Lee. Everybody is bad-mouthing Nick. Sayin' he's a pussy.

THE NARRATOR
Please stop those stupid rumors. Nick is still Nick.

BOB
If you see him, tell him Bob wants to hang. I haven't seen or heard from him since that night.

Bob walks off. Something nags.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Where was Nick? It was time to end this feud and find him.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - MORNING

Metal burning. The Narrator yanks a dry coffeepot off the burner. Ma in the chair starts to cry.

THE NARRATOR
Ma, how many times do I have to tell you NOT to try to cook anything?

MA
I can't remember anything anymore. I'm no good for anything. Nick doesn't love me. He never comes. Did I do something to make him mad?

THE NARRATOR
I'll go check on him. I'll make sure everything is okay.

MA
My heart is a mess already.

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - DAY

The door opens on a chain. Nick's crazy brown hair and pox-marked face in the slit.

NICK
What do YOU want?

THE NARRATOR
Will you let me in, please? Ma is worried about you.

NICK
Haven't you done enough damage?

The chain comes off. Tornado living room. Papers, trash, clothes on the rug.

NICK
You've ruined my life! People are talkin' shit. I can't go to the gym. They're sayin' my little brother tried to kick my ass. All because of your big mouth.

THE NARRATOR
Nick, man, whoa. I didn't do anything. I was just concerned you were going to hurt that guy.

NICK
You're not the brother I knew. Some hot shit college kid with a hoity-toity girlfriend.

THE NARRATOR
I'm working my ass off and finding out how stupid I am. That's it, man.

Silence like a verdict. Then Nick stands over him.

NICK
I will let it slide this time. But don't you ever, EVER, interrupt me in public again. You got that?

THE NARRATOR
Yes, Nick. I got that.

NICK
I'll come over tomorrow. Tell her I've been sick. She doesn't know about this shit, right?

THE NARRATOR
Of course not. She cried today because she forgot the burner was on.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Ma is laughing. Deep, heartfelt. Sionna listens to farm-outhouse Halloween stories. Peach pie waits.

MA
We were just plain hoodlums! Why don't we go to church anymore?

THE NARRATOR
You always say you don't want to. I'll take you if you want.

MA
Yes, I do. Let's go next Sunday.

The door opens. Nick walks in. Ma nearly falls out of her chair.

MA
Oh My God! It's you Nick! Where have you been hiding?

NICK
Just busy working and crap like that. This one been treatin' you all right?

Sionna comes from the kitchen with plates, holds a hand out.

SIONNA
I'm Sionna. You must be Nick. Nice to meet you, I've heard so much about you.

NICK
Uh, yea, likewise.

SIONNA
You're just in time for pie.

NICK
No thanks. I can't eat that crap. So, I hear you're a university lady. What are you gonna do when you get done?

SIONNA
College professor, if I can get my student teaching done and write a decent thesis.

NICK
Sometimes all that school just doesn't pay off. It's even harder for a woman.

SIONNA
In the last forty years, bachelor's degrees earned by female students have risen from about 35 to 55 percent. There are more females in college today than males. You can't keep a good woman down.

She sets her plate down, still polite.

SIONNA (CONT'D)
Women cannot afford to stay at home. Diversity helps us learn.

She is gone again before he can spit. Ma looks at Sionna like the second coming. Nick's smile goes thin and snake-like.

NICK
Someone in this room has a job tomorrow. Doctor Sionna.

At the threshold he grabs the Narrator's shirt and whispers close.

NICK (CONT'D)
Take care of Ma.

THE NARRATOR
Nick, you know I will.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Saturday. Nick pops up, car keys, and says he will take Ma to St. Patrick's for four o'clock. The Narrator's jaw drops. Ma flutters about what to wear.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick, at church? You don't just go from scary mother to escorting Mom to mass.

They leave in the burnt-orange Trans Am.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Homework, chips, bed. Sionna looks at him with a quizzical nod.

SIONNA
Your brother is sure suddenly into taking care of your mother, isn't he?

THE NARRATOR
Does seem kind of weird, doesn't it?

SIONNA
I can only hope I have a son or daughter like you. The circle of life.

THE NARRATOR
Do you want to have children someday?

SIONNA
I'm too busy right now. I'm going on 30 though.

They mess around and fall asleep. Last normal night.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

September. A feeling of dread on the stairs. The Narrator opens the door on complete quiet. No Ma. No purse. No coat. The answering machine blinks red.

MACHINE VOICE
You have 5 missed calls.

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)
Mr. Olson, this is Officer Mike Dupuis from the Milwaukee County Sheriff's Department.

A nurse's voice follows, Frodo Hospital. He copies a number with a shaking hand. He cannot reach Nick. He grabs his bike.

INT. POLICE STATION - DAY

Ancient stone, 1898 plaque. A wall of sound. OFFICER DUPUIS, big, graying hair, thin mustache.

OFFICER DUPUIS
Thanks for coming down, Mr. Olson. There has been a serious accident. Your brother, Nicholas Olson, and your mother, Mrs. Olivia Olson. Last night about 8 p.m. they were hit broadside at Werner and Lowe. Your brother is in serious condition at Frodo. Your mother passed away at the scene.

The rest is blur.

INT. HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

Scrubs, beeping, urine and chemical smell. Through high glass: Nick's erratic fire-colored hair, a white bandage, wires, machines.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Punctured right lung. Broken right femur. Toxicology pending. They think Nick may have been drunk.

THE NARRATOR
No. I'm really not up to seeing him. Sorry.

He walks out.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Ma's empty chair. The afghan. The goddamn pills. The Narrator stares, then breaks, a three-year-old bawling, pulling his own hair.

THE NARRATOR
Die you fucking jackass. Die. Die.

Sionna arrives later and holds him.

SIONNA
Just let it out. We'll figure it all out. Don't worry.

EXT. CEMETERY - DAY

Few people. Apartment neighbors, old coworkers, Bob solemn. Light maple lid. The Narrator throws a handful of dirt. Muted thunk.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
One day you're talking to her, the next day you're putting her in a box. I would be the only son there. I reluctantly threw a handful of dirt.

INT. HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

Five days after. Nick asking for him. The Narrator comes alone. Nick looks like shit: sallow yellow skin, black and purple bruising, oxygen tube, chest tube, right leg a huge limb under the spread. Crazy hair above the mattress.

NICK
Hey, bro.

THE NARRATOR
Hey.

NICK
I guess I really fucked up, man. Can't believe Ma's gone.

Tears on the bloodshot face. The Narrator spits facts.

THE NARRATOR
They took care of it. The mass was nice. Plot at the top of the hill at St. Mary's.

NICK
I didn't mean to have 3 Jack and Cokes before I remembered church. The cops are saying vehicular manslaughter. I was almost killed too. Why would I want to hurt Ma? Like it was all my fault.

THE NARRATOR
Whose fault was it, Nick?

NICK
You think I meant to kill her? You son of a bitch.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
He can look as mean as he wants. He is trapped. He cannot hurt me.

THE NARRATOR
Think about it, Nick.

He turns and leaves Nick's face twisted into rainbows and roadkill.

EXT. CEMETERY - DAY

Late fall sun on a new marker. MOTHER: OLIVIA ANNE OLSON. AUGUST 28, 1933 TO SEPTEMBER 7, 1998. I HAVE FOUGHT THE GOOD FIGHT. The Narrator drips onto new grass.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Yes, you did, Ma. You did keep the faith. I wonder where dad ever ended up.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - NIGHT

Busy. The Narrator is head pizza maker. TONY, older, no family left here, watches. TOMMY, delivery guy, stares at a map.

THE NARRATOR
Jesus, Tommy, it's Mr. Jones, 6:00 p.m., Friday, half pepperoni, half mushroom, black olive.

TOMMY
If you're so goddamn smart, you go deliver it.

Tony tosses the Narrator car keys.

TONY
Go on. Get out of here.

The Narrator is back in twenty. Three-dollar tip. Tony hits his shoulder.

TONY
Man, that was quick. How would you like to do more deliveries for me?

THE NARRATOR
Sure, whatever you think.

Tony talks about Ma, about how tired he looks, about help. A substitute son. The Narrator, fatherless, motherless, brotherless, takes it.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

Harsh fall, early snow. BOB walks in, pupils huge, animated.

BOB
Little brother. What the hell is going on with Nick? The cops are hounding him. They might lock him up.

THE NARRATOR
He accidentally killed one person.

BOB
He's your brother, man. Nick would fucking kill somebody if they locked him up.

THE NARRATOR
Oh, like he already did? I'm not okay with Nick. I'm not okay with Ma being dead. I'm not okay with anything.

Bob backs up, hands in the air.

BOB
I am so sorry, man. If you need anything, you'll know where to find me.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

Earlier, overlapping time: NICK in the hospital bed, sallow, tubes, staring at the ceiling.

NICK (V.O.)
That little bitch. It's all that little bitch's fault. What about my pain and suffering? Like it means nothing at all.

INT. COURTROOM - DAY

A fat woman judge, brown hair, big glasses. NICK in a black pinstripe suit, shoes scuffed, palms wet. DANIEL DAWSON beside him, silent, blond going sixty. Drug dealers and punks go first.

NICK (V.O.)
Three kinds of people. Leaders like me. Dipshits like these. Invisible people like my brother. He had one fucking job. Take care of ma. If she hadn't stepped in, everything would have been okay.

The judge reads a blood-alcohol number. Nick says yes and no. Involuntary manslaughter. A jury later. Up to ten years, or a year if they are kind.

EXT. MILWAUKEE SIDEWALK - DAY

Memory bleeds over the gray street. A big man in a blue polyester puffy coat hauls a small boy. YOUNG NICK, about eight, rubber boots skimming cement, hand crushed.

The memory-father stops, bloodshot, spit flying, then shoves the boy up a stairwell. A belt snaps. The boy looks in a dresser mirror at red welts.

NICK (V.O.)
What comes around goes around. It was coming around all right. I need to fix the root of the problem. Her.

EXT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - DUSK

Dead plants in pots. Nick knocks hard. Sionna opens a crack, pale, light-blue eyes, hippie sandals, loose clothes. A gray cat sulks toward the kitchen.

SIONNA
Nick? I never would have expected you here.

NICK
Can I talk to you?

He steps onto the wood. She offers the ratty couch. He refuses.

NICK
You're the reason I'm probably going to jail. You know that?

SIONNA
What makes you think I'm responsible for you going to jail?

He inches up, Paul Bunyan over her. She tries to pass. He walks her into the hallway wall.

NICK
If it wasn't for you messing around with my brother, Ma would be alive. Things would be perfect.

SIONNA
Nick, calm down. This really has nothing to do with me.

NICK
How about paying for my hospital and lawyer bills? You got 20 grand?

He grabs her neck with his left hand. Squeaking. His right hand finds a Swiss Army knife. He flicks it open in front of her face.

NICK
Break it off with him tonight. I'll be back in one week. I better see some fucking cash.

He sets the edge between collarbone and breast.

NICK (CONT'D)
If you leak one word, I have lots of friends. They don't give a shit if your parents are alive or dead.

The front door hits the wall.

THE NARRATOR
Holy shit, Nick, what the fuck are you doing?

The Narrator grabs for the arm. They struggle.

The knife hits her hard. Red flies. Weird noises. Nick's right elbow smashes back into his brother's head.

NICK (V.O.)
I got to get out of here.

He wipes the blade on her shirt as she sinks. He walks fast into the dark.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

The Narrator against the wall, lowering her. Pale going paler. He rips the flannel, finds the wound near the left shoulder, stacks both hands and smashes down. Sirens coming.

THE NARRATOR
Hurry up. Hurry up.

Paramedics take over. A cop pulls him into the front room. Blood on his shirt, hands, pants.

THE NARRATOR
Her name is Sionna. Sionna McAllister. Nick Olson is my brother. He stabbed her and ran.

Beeping on the monitor. A heartbeat. They take her. He does not give a shit about Nick.

INT. LOWELL'S RESTAURANT - DAY

Years later. Seattle. Second-floor window, gray clouds on the Olympics, a ferry on Puget Sound. THE NARRATOR, early forties now, broader shoulders, cleaner dark coat, Midwest face weathered quieter, stares at a cinnamon roll like a halo. Third coffee.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Seven years since my life stopped being what it was. Seven years of hatred for my brother. They told me if I hadn't applied pressure she would have died in that hallway. Sionna's savior. Right.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - DAY

A Sunday in February after the stabbing, memory. Sionna in a rocking chair, head hanging, blank zombie eyes, none of the old shine. A large foam pillow has lived under her left arm for weeks.

THE NARRATOR
Sionna? What's going on? Are you depressed?

SIONNA
I guess I'm a little depressed. Thinking about seeing a therapist.

THE NARRATOR
I feel like you're blaming me. You know it had nothing to do with me, right?

SIONNA
I can't feel me. I can't feel myself anymore.

He squats to her eye line.

THE NARRATOR
You are safe. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA
As long as there is any doubt, I can't go on with you. Maybe we just need a break. Go to Mom and Dad's for a while.

THE NARRATOR
Stay. Stay here with me. Please?

She walks into the bedroom. He leaves for work an hour early, fingers slipping off a cliff.

EXT. SEATTLE STREET - DAY

Pike Place. Tourists, flowers, the original Starbucks line. The Narrator, early forties, dark coat, walks through the Seattle freeze. A girl's dark brown hair flashes. He thinks Sionna and keeps walking.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I now had my own chain. Tony's Boy's Pizza. I used his name in honor.

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Warm. BOZO the cat, orange and white, dismissive on the couch. The Narrator eats, reads, drifts.

He dreams a winding dirt road, magnified green hills. An Indian sage in a white wrap walks beside him. A dirty pizza box on the road. The sage is disappointed.

SAGE
Stop judging things. The pizza box is here only when we view it. All things are an illusion. Start living the truth.

The sage is gone. The box is gone. The Narrator wakes smiling, writes it all down beside the cat.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I had to change. I had to find forgiveness.

He books a flight to Milwaukee before he can lose the feeling.

EXT. KIRK STREET - DAY

Nothing changed in ten years. Brick fortress, black fire escape, dumpsters instead of dented cans. The Narrator, early forties, broader, stands in the alley that was a playland.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I wanted to cry for all the time I wasted being Nick's little pawn. I was past it. I was a grown man.

INT. DELI - DAY

A Reuben, fries, iced tea. Then BOB at the window, greasy burger, hair graying and thinning, thicker waist. He slams the burger down and cackles.

BOB
Holy shit. Is that you, bro? You came back, you son of a bitch.

THE NARRATOR
Bob. What the hell is going on?

BOB
Look at you. Big shoulders. Ivy League talk. You here to visit Nick?

THE NARRATOR
I haven't seen him for 8 years. I need to let the shit go.

BOB
I don't think I could forgive him myself. I used to visit. I couldn't do it no more.

THE NARRATOR
It's not your responsibility. He made his own choices.

BOB
You are a wise man. I took over his apartment. I'm at the factory now.

A bear hug. Bob almost cries.

BOB (CONT'D)
I'm so proud of you, man. You got out of here.

EXT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - DAY

Smaller, grayer. Naked porch. The Narrator sits in a rental Camry like a stalker.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I loved her so much. I just want her to be happy. I am doing the right thing by leaving her alone.

He looks in the rearview.

THE NARRATOR
Well, you can't look back, man, because you are moving forward.

EXT. GREEN BAY PRISON - DAY

Oppressive brick. Gravel lot. The Narrator walks toward metal detectors and burly men.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
No arguing. No blaming. No judgment. I have evolved even if he hasn't.

INT. PRISON VISITING ROOM - DAY

Plexiglass. Phones. The Narrator waits. Then NICK comes: older, scrawny beside his former self, hair graying at the sides and thinning on top, lined forehead, watery sad bags under the eyes, the same scars on chin and eyebrow like fossils.

NICK
Hi, bro. Glad you came.

THE NARRATOR
Hi, Nick. Good to see you finally. How are you holding up?

NICK
Not a cakewalk. Looking pretty good for getting out early. Parole hearing in 2 years.

THE NARRATOR
Well, that's good for you, Nick. I would appreciate it if you would just listen to me. Okay?

NICK
Yeah. Okay, man. Okay.

THE NARRATOR
I am no longer interested in seeing you. You're an asshole that killed my mother. You're an asshole that held me down so you could have the big money. Mostly, you're an asshole for trying to kill the love of my life.

Nick's eyes fill.

THE NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Now that you know, I have to add this. I forgive you. I forgive you because it sets me free.

The tension of thirty years lets go. He is almost smiling.

NICK
I'm so sorry. I really didn't expect this. You're so right. I am a huge asshole. I feel so bad about Ma and Sionna. You're more than I ever was. I deserve to live in here.

A guard signals time.

THE NARRATOR
I still believe in you. I still love you for being my brother. Do you hear me?

NICK
I hear you, man. I love you too. What are you doing now?

THE NARRATOR
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. You always said I couldn't do it. I did it, Nick.

NICK
President Olson. That's fucking amazing. I am so proud of you, man. I love you.

The far guard moves him. Nick smiles.

NICK
Bye.

The Narrator hangs up and watches him shuffled down the hall. He sits with the yellow receiver, exonerated.

EXT. WISCONSIN HIGHWAY - DAY

Rolling hills past the rental car. The weight of thirty years gone.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Somewhere, she's out there. She's happy. I have to believe that. I hope she believes that I'm happy too.

INT. MILWAUKEE AIRPORT - DAY

A boarding queue. The Narrator, early forties, broader, dark coat, floats above the throng. Nick's teary voice still in his head. An attendant scans the pass. He looks down at the name.

NICK (V.O.)
President Olson.

THE NARRATOR
That's me. President Peter Olson.

He whispers it to himself, almost smiling, and walks down the jetway.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
This has been Nick and me.

The jetway mouth frames him small against the bright oval of the plane door, then takes him.

FADE OUT.

THE END